! Changed by HYPERION: reduce.py kept only the 111 records P14.T49.d reads.
!
!              SIX-CHARACTER REFERENCE-DATE CODES
!
!
!  Letters         Reference     Numbers
!  __________________________________________________________________
!
!    g      Glenn Research Center              Month/year calculated
!
!    j      NIST-JANAF Thermochemical          Month/year of table
!           Tables. Chase,1998
!
!    tpis   Thermodynamic Properties of        Year of volume
!           Individual Substances. Gurvich,
!           1978,1979,1982,1989,1991,1996
!
!    n      TRC Thermodynamic Tables, NIST     Month/year of table
!
!    bar    Barin:Thermochemical Data of       Year of volume
!           Pure Substances. Barin,1989
!
!    coda   CODATA Key Values for              Year of volume
!           Thermodynamics. Cox,1989
!
!    srd    Standard Reference Data            Year of J.Phys.Chem.Ref.
!                                              Data journal
!
!  Updated version of thermo.inp released with standalone CEA program:
!  Original version dated 9/09/04, reported in NASA TP-2001-210959,
!     "CAP: A Computer Code for Generating Tabular Thermodynamic Functions from NASA Lewis Coefficients"
!      by Michael J. Zehe, Sanford Gordon, and Bonnie J. McBride
!      Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, Ohio
!
!  Updates:
!
!  Chris Snyder (NASA GRC), September 9, 2021
!    Update species T ranges, many arbitrarily set to 200K, but data is only
!    fit to 300K, so reset most T ranges to 300K.  Hopefully fix issues at
!    sub-300K CEA ops in NPSS
!
!  Chris Snyder (NASA GRC), August 16, 2021
!    Revision of thermo, also added JP-4, JP-5, JP-7, RP-1, and Paraffin
!    Also, to enable inerts, added inert versions of O, O2, H, H2, CH4, C2H4,
!    C10H8 (Naphthalene), JP-?, RP-1 & Jet-A.  The various hydrocarbon (HC)
!    species are to enable users to "lock" some of the various HC species to
!    simulate incomplete combustion.  (Allow the fuel to break down a little
!    and absorb heat, but not combust with O2.) In standalone CEA runs, it was
!    found that CH4, C2H4, C10H8 captured fuel breakdown energy characteristics
!    (1st order) for a range of H to C ratioes.
!
!  Chris Snyder - update data for FeCL3 (unit conversion error in data) 1/2019
!
!
!                   ORDER OF SPECIES
!
!         1)  Gaseous products/reactants
!
!         2)  Condensed products/reactants
!
!         3)  Gaseous/condensed reactants only (starts with "Air")
!
!
